            a standard SLDS just updates latent states and the continuous dynamics follow from this
            we can't learn a B matrix as it can only ever assume that the action it took got it to the
            state it expected to be in next.
            
            '''
            # we get next state of the latent by applying relevant slice (given action we selected) of transition matrix to current state 
            next_state = np,dot(B[...,chosen_action_id], qs) # get the relevant slice of the B matrix and update current state
            next_observation = np.dot(A, next_state)
            # note that the problem here is that if B matrix is unknown, how do we know what our next state truly is!
            
            
            
        if non_standard_SLDS:
            '''
            if we have any kind of uncertainty in our B matrix (i.e. if we are learning it from flat)
            then we have to be able to infer our state after we apply an action (as we don't know where we 
            will end up). i.e. we need to map from continuous space to a categorical observation. 
            This is a bit more like Parr and Friston as now we want to incorporate a notion of a 'link function'.
            
            To do this we put Gaussian likelihoods around each of the fixed points in continuous space and 
            then we just get the posterior probability over which Gaussian is most likely to have been responsible for producing 
            the datapoint (this is the E-step in EM).
            
            '''

            # Compute the unnormalized responsibilities (likelihoods * priors) assuming equal priors
            responsibilities = [multivariate_normal.pdf(data_point, mean=mean, cov=covariance) for fixed_point_mean in fixed_point_means]
            
            # Normalize the responsibilities to get probabilities
            probabilities = softmax(responsibilities)
            
            # argmax to get the most likely discrete state
            state = np.argmax(responsibilities)
            init_state = np.eye(len(fps))[state]
            
            # step through pymdp A matrix to get the next obervation
            next_observation = np.dot(A, init_state)
            
            # now this alows us to update the B matrix
